      "author": "MovieGuys",
      "content": "\"Apex\" gives the strong impression it hopes the location will carry the film. To be fair, the exotic wilderness of outback Australia is an interesting setting. That said, its window dressing on a film that's lacking in substance and pacing. \r\n\r\nThe first shortcoming is Apex's key characterisations feel shallow. You never get any real insight into the mind of the deranged serial killer or, for that matter, his would-be victim. Both remain distant and unknown. This is a major shortcoming. What starts out as a pursuit thriller which supports a more exposition-driven story and bare-bones characterisations quickly turns into a psychological thriller minus the psychological depth it needs to carry it forward. The abrupt finale really demonstrates the confusion as to what Apex is supposed to be. \r\n\r\nSadly, I can't say a lot of positives about acting either. The lead character's performance feels wooden and forced, which may well be down to character's limtiations in the script. The character of the \"Brit/Aussie\" serial killer is a little overcooked. He's what non Australian's think Australians are. I can say this wth confidence, as someone who lived in Sydney, for a number of years. \r\n\r\nAction is well done and creative. Both actors have the physicality to carry the outback action convincingly, which does add a sense of realism. \r\n\r\nIn summary, whilst not unwatchable, I found myself viewing Apex in stops and starts over a couple of days, which is never a good sign. Its failure to decide what it wants to be, constructing a compelling story that reflects that decision is, in my estimation, its single biggest shortcoming. On the upside, action is well done, and yes, the location is compelling. A marginal 6/10 from me.",
      "rating": 6,
      "createdAt": "2026-07-14T04:28:18Z"
    }
